(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["strings",{"l:strings/python-strings-real-costs-counts-and-canonical-s":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A Python \u003ccode>str\u003c/code> is an immutable array of code points, so indexing and \u003ccode>len\u003c/code> are O(1) but every slice, \u003ccode>+\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>replace\u003c/code> builds a new string. Most \"string problems\" are really counting, window, two-pointer, DP or parsing problems; the genuinely string-specific tools are in the next cards. For \"are these the same up to rearrangement or relabelling?\" compare \u003cb>counts\u003c/b> or a \u003cb>canonical shape\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Storage\u003c/b> (PEP 393): each string stores every code point in 1, 2 or 4 bytes, chosen by its widest character. \u003ccode>s[i]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>len(s)\u003c/code> are O(1); \u003ccode>len\u003c/code> counts code points, not bytes or visible characters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Immutability\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>s[i:j]\u003c/code> copies j - i characters. \u003ccode>res += piece\u003c/code> in a loop is O(total^2) in general (CPython sometimes extends in place when nothing else references the string; do not rely on it). Collect pieces in a list and \u003ccode>\"\".join\u003c/code> them once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hashing\u003c/b>: a string's hash is computed once and cached in the object, so repeated dict lookups with the same key object are cheap; equality is O(L) at worst.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Searching\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>in\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>find\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>count\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>replace\u003c/code> use CPython's fastsearch, a Boyer-Moore-Horspool hybrid that skips ahead on mismatches, switching to the Two-Way algorithm for long needles so the worst case stays linear. For one search, \u003ccode>pat in text\u003c/code> beats any hand-written loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>collections.Counter(s)\u003c/code> counts in C; for lowercase ASCII a 26-slot list indexed by \u003ccode>ord(ch) - 97\u003c/code> is smaller and lets you compare counts in O(26).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>The statement says\u003c/th>\u003cth>Usually means\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>anagram, same letters, rearrange\u003c/td>\u003ctd>counts\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>longest or shortest substring with a condition\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sliding window\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>two strings, subsequence, edits\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DP on prefixes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>nested brackets, encoded repeats, expressions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>stack or parser\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>palindromic substring\u003c/td>\u003ctd>expand around centres\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>find, occurrences, period, prefix equals suffix\u003c/td>\u003ctd>prefix function, Z-function or rolling hash\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\n\ndef counts26(s):\n    \"\"\"Letter counts for lowercase ASCII; compare two of these in O(26).\"\"\"\n    c = [0] * 26\n    for ch in s:\n        c[ord(ch) - 97] += 1\n    return c\n\n\ndef shape(s):\n    \"\"\"First-occurrence pattern: equal shapes mean a one-to-one letter mapping exists.\"\"\"\n    first = {}\n    return tuple(first.setdefault(ch, len(first)) for ch in s)\n\n\ndef same_letters(a, b):\n    return Counter(a) == Counter(b)\n\n\ndef build(pieces):\n    return \"\".join(pieces)             # one O(total) copy, never += in a loop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Is there a one-to-one letter mapping from \"noon\" to \"deed\", and from \"noon\" to \"dead\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">shape(\"noon\"): n is new (0), o is new (1), o seen (1), n seen (0)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>noon  -&gt;  (\u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>, 1, 0)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">shape(\"deed\"): d is 0, e is 1, e again 1, d again 0. Same shape, so n-&gt;d, o-&gt;e works both ways\u003c/p>\u003cpre>deed  -&gt;  (0, 1, 1, 0)      equal to noon's shape\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">shape(\"dead\"): d 0, e 1, a is new (2), d 0. The shapes differ at position 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>dead  -&gt;  (0, 1, \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>, 0)      noon has 1 here: o would map to e and a\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Quadratic building\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>s = s[:i] + c + s[i+1:]\u003c/code> inside a loop. Use a list of characters, then join.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One-way mappings\u003c/b>: checking only s to t lets \"ab\" map to \"cc\". Shapes, or two dicts, check both directions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>ASCII assumptions\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>ord(ch) - 97\u003c/code> breaks on capitals, digits and accents. Normalise with \u003ccode>casefold()\u003c/code>, and use \u003ccode>unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", s)\u003c/code> when \"é\" may arrive as one code point or two.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Identity\u003c/b>: compare strings with \u003ccode>==\u003c/code>, never \u003ccode>is\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is shape(\"abca\"), and is there a one-to-one mapping from \"abca\" to \"zyxz\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(0, 1, 2, 0). \"zyxz\" also has shape (0, 1, 2, 0), so yes: a to z, b to y, c to x.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know what Python string operations really cost and how counts and canonical shapes answer equivalence questions in O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:strings/palindromes-expanding-around-2n-1-centres":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every palindrome is symmetric around a \u003cb>centre\u003c/b>: a character (odd length) or the gap between two characters (even length), so a string of length n has \u003cb>2n - 1 centres\u003c/b>. From each centre, grow outwards while the two ends match. The maximal palindrome at each centre gives the longest one, and every successful growth step is one more palindrome, so the same loop also counts them.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why expanding is enough\u003c/b>: if s[lo..hi] is a palindrome, so is s[lo+1..hi-1], with the same centre. The palindromes around one centre are therefore nested, and growing until the first mismatch finds all of them: a maximal span of length L contains (L + 1) // 2 palindromes centred there.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Cost\u003c/b>: each centre costs O(1 + radius). The total is O(n^2) in the worst case (a string of one repeated letter, where every centre grows to the nearest edge) and close to O(n) on typical text, with O(1) extra space. Compare: checking all O(n^2) substrings with \u003ccode>t == t[::-1]\u003c/code> costs O(n^3); a DP table of \"is s[i..j] a palindrome\" costs O(n^2) time \u003cb>and\u003c/b> O(n^2) memory.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Manacher in outline\u003c/b>: keep the palindrome that reaches furthest right, [l, r]. For a new centre i inside it, the mirror centre j = l + r - i has already been expanded, and inside [l, r] the text around i mirrors the text around j, so i's radius is at least min(radius[j], r - i). Start expanding from there; every comparison that succeeds pushes r to the right, and r never moves left, so the total work is O(n). Interleaving a separator (\u003ccode>#a#b#a#\u003c/code>) turns every centre into a character so one loop handles both parities.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a whole string, \u003ccode>s == s[::-1]\u003c/code> is the fastest check in Python (C speed). Use two pointers when characters must be skipped or one deletion is allowed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def expand(s, lo, hi):\n    \"\"\"Grow around the centre (lo, hi) while the ends match; return the widest palindrome as (lo, hi).\"\"\"\n    while lo &gt;= 0 and hi &lt; len(s) and s[lo] == s[hi]:\n        lo -= 1\n        hi += 1\n    return lo + 1, hi - 1                 # an empty span (hi &lt; lo) when even and s[lo] != s[hi]\n\n\ndef centre_spans(s):\n    \"\"\"The maximal palindrome around each of the 2n - 1 centres.\"\"\"\n    for c in range(len(s)):\n        yield expand(s, c, c)             # odd length: centred on s[c]\n        if c + 1 &lt; len(s):\n            yield expand(s, c, c + 1)     # even length: centred on the gap after s[c]\n\n\nspans = list(centre_spans(\"cbbcabac\"))\nlo, hi = max(spans, key=lambda span: span[1] - span[0])    # longest: s[3:8] == \"cabac\"\ncount = sum((hi - lo + 2) // 2 for lo, hi in spans)        # all palindromic substrings: 12\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>s = \"cbbcabac\" (indices 0 to 7). Five of the fifteen centres, including the two that matter.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Centre on s[0] = \"c\": s[-1] does not exist, so the span is just \"c\": 1 palindrome\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns      \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> b b c a b a c\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Gap between s[1] and s[2]: b == b gives \"bb\"; then s[0] == s[3] (c == c) gives \"cbbc\"; then the left end runs out\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns      \u003cmark>c b b c\u003c/mark> a b a c      2 palindromes: bb, cbbc\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Gap between s[2] and s[3]: b != c, so nothing: 0 palindromes\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns       c b \u003cmark>b c\u003c/mark> a b a c\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Centre on s[5] = \"b\": a == a gives \"aba\", c == c gives \"cabac\", then s[2] = b vs s[8]: out of range\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns       c b b \u003cmark>c a b a c\u003c/mark>      3 palindromes: b, aba, cabac\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">After all 15 centres: longest is \"cabac\" (3..7); count = 8 single letters + bb + cbbc + aba + cabac = 12\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns       c b b \u003cmark>c a b a c\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Odd centres only\u003c/b>: misses \"bb\" and \"cbbc\". Every problem needs both parities (or Manacher's separator trick).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Off by one on return\u003c/b>: the loop exits one step past the last match, so return (lo + 1, hi - 1).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Slicing inside the loop\u003c/b>: building s[lo:hi+1] at every step turns O(n^2) into O(n^3); keep indices and slice once at the end.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Palindromic subsequence is different\u003c/b>: characters need not be adjacent, which is an interval DP, not centre expansion.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How many palindromic substrings does \"aaa\" have, and which centres produce them?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Six. Odd centres give a, a, a and \"aaa\" (the middle centre expands twice); the two gaps give \"aa\" each.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to find and count palindromic substrings by expanding around 2n - 1 centres, and why Manacher's mirror trick makes it linear.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:strings/rolling-hash-and-rabin-karp":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Treat a string as a number in base B: hash(c0 c1 ... c(L-1)) = c0 * B^(L-1) + ... + c(L-1), modulo a large prime M. Sliding the window one character costs O(1): remove the leading character's term, multiply by B, add the new character. With \u003cb>prefix hashes\u003c/b> any substring's hash is O(1) after O(n) preprocessing. Equal strings always have equal hashes; unequal strings collide only rarely if M is large and B is \u003cb>random\u003c/b>, so compare the actual text on every hit and the answer is exact.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Sliding\u003c/b>: if h is the hash of s[i:i+L], then the hash of s[i+1:i+L+1] is ((h - s[i] * B^(L-1)) * B + s[i+L]) mod M. \u003cb>Prefix hashes\u003c/b>: with H[0] = 0 and H[i+1] = H[i] * B + s[i], the hash of s[i:j] is H[j] - H[i] * B^(j-i) (mod M).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Collisions\u003c/b>: for two different strings of length L and a base chosen uniformly at random, a collision means B is a root of a nonzero polynomial of degree below L, which has fewer than L roots, so the probability is below L / M. With M = 2^61 - 1 (a Mersenne prime) and L = 10^5 that is under 10^-13 per comparison. Storing N hashes in a set is a birthday problem: expect about N^2 / (2M) colliding pairs, which is about 500 for N = 10^6 and M near 10^9. Hence: a 61-bit prime (Python's big integers make this easy), or \u003cb>double hashing\u003c/b> (two independent moduli and bases, standard in C++ and Java where 64-bit products overflow); a base drawn at run time, so nobody can craft colliding inputs (with the \"free\" modulus 2^64, a known family of strings built from the Thue-Morse sequence collides for every base); and a real comparison before reporting a match.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Cost\u003c/b>: O(n + m) expected for Rabin-Karp, but every true match is verified in O(m), so \"aaa\" in \"aaaa...\" costs O(nm); KMP and Z guarantee O(n + m). Hashing wins when you compare \u003cb>many\u003c/b> substrings: duplicates of length L, longest repeated substring (binary search on L), equality of arbitrary ranges.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>In real tools\u003c/b>: rsync's receiver sends a weak 32-bit rolling checksum (two running sums modulo 2^16, inspired by Adler-32) and a strong checksum per block; the sender slides the weak one over \u003cb>every byte offset\u003c/b> of its file and computes the strong one only for candidates. restic cuts chunks where a Rabin fingerprint of the last 64 bytes matches a pattern, so an insertion moves only nearby boundaries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\nMOD = (1 &lt;&lt; 61) - 1                       # Mersenne prime\nBASE = random.randrange(256, MOD - 1)     # random per run: no input can be crafted against it\n\n\nclass PrefixHash:\n    \"\"\"Hash of any substring s[i:j] in O(1) after O(n) preprocessing.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, s):\n        self.h = [0] * (len(s) + 1)       # h[i] = hash of s[:i]\n        self.p = [1] * (len(s) + 1)       # p[i] = BASE**i % MOD\n        for i, ch in enumerate(s):\n            self.h[i + 1] = (self.h[i] * BASE + ord(ch)) % MOD\n            self.p[i + 1] = self.p[i] * BASE % MOD\n\n    def get(self, i, j):\n        return (self.h[j] - self.h[i] * self.p[j - i]) % MOD\n\n\ndef rabin_karp(text, pat):\n    \"\"\"All start indices of pat in text. Every hash hit is checked, so the answer is exact.\"\"\"\n    n, m = len(text), len(pat)\n    if m == 0:\n        return list(range(n + 1))\n    if m &gt; n:\n        return []\n    target = h = 0\n    for k in range(m):\n        target = (target * BASE + ord(pat[k])) % MOD\n        h = (h * BASE + ord(text[k])) % MOD\n    top = pow(BASE, m - 1, MOD)           # weight of the character that leaves the window\n    out = []\n    for i in range(n - m + 1):\n        if h == target and text[i:i + m] == pat:\n            out.append(i)\n        if i + m &lt; n:\n            h = ((h - ord(text[i]) * top) * BASE + ord(text[i + m])) % MOD\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find \"159\" in \"31415926\" with a deliberately tiny modulus: B = 10, M = 13, digits as values. hash(\"159\") = 159 mod 13 = 3, and B^2 mod 13 = 9.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Window 314: 314 mod 13 = 2, not the target 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text    \u003cmark>3 1 4\u003c/mark> 1 5 9 2 6        h = 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: drop 3, add 1: (2 - 3 * 9) * 10 + 1 = -249, and -249 mod 13 = 11 (= 141 mod 13)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text    3 \u003cmark>1 4 1\u003c/mark> 5 9 2 6        h = 11\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: (11 - 1 * 9) * 10 + 5 = 25, mod 13 = 12 (= 415 mod 13)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text    3 1 \u003cmark>4 1 5\u003c/mark> 9 2 6        h = 12\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: (12 - 4 * 9) * 10 + 9 = -231, mod 13 = 3: a hit. Compare the text: \"159\" == \"159\", match at 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text    3 1 4 \u003cmark>1 5 9\u003c/mark> 2 6        h = \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide twice more: 592 gives 7, then 926 gives 3: another hit, but \"926\" != \"159\". A collision, caught by the comparison\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text    3 1 4 1 5 \u003cmark>9 2 6\u003c/mark>        h = \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   rejected\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With M = 2^61 - 1 and a random base, that second hit would essentially never happen; the comparison makes the answer exact either way.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Negative remainders\u003c/b> in C++, Java or Go: \u003ccode>(a - b) % M\u003c/code> can be negative; add M before taking the remainder. Python's \u003ccode>%\u003c/code> is already non-negative.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overflow\u003c/b>: in 64-bit languages the product of two values below 2^61 overflows; use 128-bit multiplication or two 31-bit moduli.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Skipping verification\u003c/b>, or using a small or famous modulus with a fixed base on adversarial input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Comparing hashes of different lengths\u003c/b>: with prefix hashes, \"a\" and \"\\x00a\" can collide; only compare equal-length substrings, or include the length in the key.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Python's \u003ccode>hash()\u003c/code>\u003c/b> of a slice still costs O(L), and it is randomised per process: fine within one run, never for values you store.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must the base be random, and what does verifying each hit cost in the worst case?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With a fixed base (and modulus), someone can compute two different strings with equal hashes in advance; a base chosen at run time makes any fixed pair collide with probability below L / M. Verification costs O(m) per hit, so a text full of true matches costs O(nm) in total.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to slide and query polynomial hashes in O(1), why a 61-bit prime with a random base is safe, and why every hit is verified.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:strings/kmp-and-the-prefix-function":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>pi[i] is the length of the longest \u003cb>border\u003c/b> of s[:i+1]: a proper prefix that is also a suffix. When a match of k characters fails, the pattern can resume at pi[k-1] characters instead of starting over, because that much of what you just matched is already a prefix of the pattern. The text pointer never moves backwards, so matching costs O(n + m), and pi itself is built by the same loop in O(m).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Building pi\u003c/b>: keep k = pi[i-1], the border you are trying to extend. If s[i] == s[k], the border grows to k + 1. If not, fall back to the next shorter border of the same prefix, which is pi[k-1], and try again; stop at k = 0. The borders of a string form a chain k, pi[k-1], pi[pi[k-1]-1], ..., so the fallback visits every candidate in decreasing order and the first that extends is the longest.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why O(m)\u003c/b>: k rises by at most 1 per index and every fallback lowers it by at least 1, so the total number of fallbacks is at most the total number of rises: at most m. The same argument bounds matching by O(n + m).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Matching\u003c/b>: run the identical loop with the text as input and the pattern as the thing being extended; when k reaches m, report a match at i - m + 1 and fall back to pi[m-1] so overlapping matches are found. Equivalently, compute pi of pattern + \"\\x00\" + text and report every position where pi equals m (the separator must not occur in either string).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>What else pi tells you\u003c/b>: s[:pi[n-1]] is the longest prefix that is also a suffix. p = n - pi[n-1] is the smallest period, and s is a whole repetition of a block exactly when pi[n-1] &gt; 0 and n % p == 0. The longest palindromic prefix of s is pi's last value for s + \"#\" + reversed(s).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>In real tools\u003c/b>: KMP is a worst-case guarantee and a source of structure, not what fast searchers run. CPython's \u003ccode>str.find\u003c/code> and GNU grep skip ahead with Boyer-Moore-style shift tables (CPython switches to Two-Way for long needles). Aho-Corasick is KMP generalised to a trie of many patterns, with failure links playing the role of pi; Suricata offers it as a multi-pattern matcher.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def prefix_function(s):\n    \"\"\"pi[i] = length of the longest proper prefix of s[:i + 1] that is also its suffix.\"\"\"\n    pi = [0] * len(s)\n    k = 0                                  # the border being extended\n    for i in range(1, len(s)):\n        while k &gt; 0 and s[i] != s[k]:\n            k = pi[k - 1]                  # next shorter border\n        if s[i] == s[k]:\n            k += 1\n        pi[i] = k\n    return pi\n\n\ndef kmp_search(text, pat):\n    \"\"\"All start indices of pat in text in O(n + m).\"\"\"\n    if not pat:\n        return list(range(len(text) + 1))\n    pi = prefix_function(pat)\n    out, k = [], 0                         # k = characters of pat matched so far\n    for i, ch in enumerate(text):\n        while k &gt; 0 and ch != pat[k]:\n            k = pi[k - 1]\n        if ch == pat[k]:\n            k += 1\n        if k == len(pat):\n            out.append(i - k + 1)\n            k = pi[k - 1]                  # keep going: matches may overlap\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build pi for the pattern \"abacabab\", then match it in \"abacabacabab\".\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1, 2: \"b\" cannot extend the empty border (pi 0); \"a\" matches s[0] (pi 1)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s    a b a c a b a b\npi   0 0 \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3: \"c\" != s[1] = \"b\", fall back to k = pi[0] = 0; \"c\" != s[0]: pi 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s    a b a \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> a b a b\npi   0 0 1 \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4, 5, 6: a, b, a each extend the border: \"a\", \"ab\", \"aba\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s    a b a c \u003cmark>a b a\u003c/mark> b\npi   0 0 1 0 \u003cmark>1 2 3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 7: \"b\" != s[3] = \"c\", so fall back to k = pi[2] = 1; \"b\" == s[1]: k = 2. The border is \"ab\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s    a b a c a b a \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>\npi   0 0 1 0 1 2 3 \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Matching: text[0..6] = \"abacaba\" equals the pattern's first 7 characters: k = 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text  \u003cmark>a b a c a b a\u003c/mark> c a b a b\npat   \u003cmark>a b a c a b a\u003c/mark> b\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">text[7] = \"c\" but pat[7] = \"b\": mismatch. Fall back to k = pi[6] = 3, since \"aba\" is both a suffix of what matched and a prefix of pat\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text  a b a c \u003cmark>a b a\u003c/mark> c a b a b\npat           \u003cmark>a b a\u003c/mark> c a b a b\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Compare the same text character again: text[7] = \"c\" == pat[3]: k = 4. The text pointer never went back\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text  a b a c a b a \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> a b a b\npat           a b a \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> a b a b\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">text[8..11] = \"abab\" extend k to 8 = m: match at 11 - 8 + 1 = 4, then k = pi[7] = 2 for overlapping matches\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text  a b a c \u003cmark>a b a c a b a b\u003c/mark>\npat           \u003cmark>a b a c a b a b\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>k = pi[k] instead of pi[k - 1]\u003c/b>: pi is indexed by the last position of the matched prefix, which is k - 1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>if\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>while\u003c/code>\u003c/b> for the fallback: one step back is not always enough.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No fallback after a full match\u003c/b>: the next comparison reads pat[m] and raises IndexError, or overlapping matches are lost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A separator that occurs in the data\u003c/b> when using pattern + separator + text; pick a character outside the alphabet, or run the two-phase version above.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the prefix function of \"aabaaab\", and what are its smallest period and longest border?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>[0, 1, 0, 1, 2, 2, 3]. The longest border is \"aab\" (length 3), so the smallest period is 7 - 3 = 4; since 7 % 4 != 0, the string is not a whole repetition of a block.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to build the prefix function with its fallback chain, match in O(n + m), and read borders and periods from it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:strings/the-z-function-and-its-z-box":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>z[i] is the length of the longest common prefix of s and the suffix starting at i. The algorithm keeps the \u003cb>Z-box\u003c/b> [l, r): the rightmost segment known to equal a prefix of s. For an i inside the box, s[i:r] is a copy of s[i-l:r-l], so z[i] starts at min(r - i, z[i - l]) for free; only characters beyond r are compared, and r never moves left, so the whole array costs O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Three cases\u003c/b> for each i from 1 to n - 1:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>i is outside the box\u003c/b> (i &gt;= r): compare from scratch, s[0] with s[i], s[1] with s[i+1], ...\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inside, and the copied value fits\u003c/b> (z[i - l] &lt; r - i): the answer is exactly z[i - l]; the mismatch that stopped it is also inside the box. No comparisons succeed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inside, and the copy reaches the box edge\u003c/b> (z[i - l] &gt;= r - i): start at r - i and keep comparing past r.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Whenever the match for i ends beyond r, the box becomes [i, i + z[i]).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why O(n)\u003c/b>: every successful comparison moves r one step to the right (it compares s at a position at or beyond r), r only grows, and it stops at n. Each i adds at most one failed comparison. Total: O(n).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Uses\u003c/b>: matching (compute z of pattern + separator + text; a position with z equal to m is a match); the sum of all z values plus n is the total length of all prefix-suffix matches (LeetCode 2223); a period p of s exists where p + z[p] == n. The prefix function and the Z-function carry the same information and can be converted into each other in O(n); many people find the Z-box easier to reason about, while KMP's form streams naturally (it never needs the text in advance).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def z_function(s):\n    \"\"\"z[i] = length of the longest common prefix of s and s[i:]; z[0] is left as 0.\"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    z = [0] * n\n    l = r = 0                                  # Z-box: s[l:r] == s[0:r - l]\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if i &lt; r:\n            z[i] = min(r - i, z[i - l])        # copy from inside the box\n        while i + z[i] &lt; n and s[z[i]] == s[i + z[i]]:\n            z[i] += 1                          # compare only past what is known\n        if i + z[i] &gt; r:\n            l, r = i, i + z[i]\n    return z\n\n\ndef z_search(text, pat, sep=\"\\x00\"):\n    \"\"\"Start indices of pat in text; sep must occur in neither string.\"\"\"\n    z = z_function(pat + sep + text)\n    m = len(pat)\n    return [i - m - 1 for i in range(m + 1, len(z)) if z[i] &gt;= m]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>s = \"abaababc\". Box [l, r) shown with brackets below the string.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1, 2 are outside any box: \"b\" vs \"a\" fails (z 0); \"a\" matches s[0], then \"a\" vs \"b\" fails (z 1). Box [2, 3)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i     0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns     a b a a b a b c\nz     0 0 \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3: outside the box. \"aba\" matches s[0:3], then s[6] = \"b\" vs s[3] = \"a\" fails: z = 3, box [3, 6)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s     a b a \u003cmark>a b a\u003c/mark> b c\nbox         [3     6)\nz     0 0 1 \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4: inside. Copy z[4 - 3] = z[1] = 0, which is less than r - i = 2: z = 0 with no comparisons\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s     a b a a \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark> a b c\nbox         [3     6)\nz     0 0 1 3 \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 5: inside. z[5 - 3] = z[2] = 1 reaches the box edge (r - i = 1), so start at 1 and compare past r: s[1] = \"b\" == s[6], then \"a\" vs \"c\" fails. z = 2, box [5, 7)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s     a b a a b \u003cmark>a b\u003c/mark> c\nbox               [5  7)\nz     0 0 1 3 0 \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 6: inside. z[1] = 0 &lt; r - i = 1, so z = 0. i = 7: outside, \"c\" vs \"a\" fails, z = 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s     a b a a b a \u003cmark>b c\u003c/mark>\nz     0 0 1 3 0 2 \u003cmark>0 0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the min with r - i\u003c/b>: copying z[i - l] blindly claims matches beyond the box that were never checked.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Updating the box unconditionally\u003c/b>: when i + z[i] does not pass r, replacing the box moves r to the left, forgets matched text and breaks the O(n) argument. Update only when i + z[i] &gt; r.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>z[0]\u003c/b>: the template leaves it 0; some problems want n there (2223 counts the whole string), so add it explicitly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The separator\u003c/b>: as with KMP, it must not occur in the pattern or text, and searching with \u003ccode>z[i] &gt;= m\u003c/code> relies on it to stop matches at length m.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">For s = \"aaaa\", what is the Z-array, and how many character comparisons succeed in total?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>[0, 3, 2, 1]. Only i = 1 compares from scratch (3 successes, then the end of the string); i = 2 and i = 3 lie inside the box [1, 4) and copy min(r - i, z[i - l]) = 2 and 1 without any successful comparison. Three successes in total.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how the Z-box lets you compute every prefix-match length in O(n), and how to use it for matching.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:strings/parsing-tokenising-and-length-prefixed-encodings":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Parse in two layers: a \u003cb>tokeniser\u003c/b> turns characters into tokens (numbers, words, symbols), and a parser or \u003cb>state machine\u003c/b> consumes tokens with an explicit state such as \"before sign, in digits, done\". To store a list of strings in one string, a delimiter alone fails as soon as a value contains the delimiter; prefix each value with its \u003cb>length\u003c/b> (or escape the delimiter) and decoding becomes unambiguous.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Tokenise first.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>s.split()\u003c/code> (no argument) splits on runs of whitespace and drops empty strings, which is exactly \"words\" for most problems. For richer input, one regular expression with named alternatives, scanned with \u003ccode>re.finditer\u003c/code>, yields typed tokens in one pass; a hand-written index loop does the same with full control. Each character is examined a constant number of times, so tokenising is O(n).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>State machines.\u003c/b> Write the states down before coding: for a signed integer they are leading spaces, optional sign, digits, stop. Each character either moves to the next state or ends the parse. Clamping, overflow and \"stop at the first non-digit\" then become explicit transitions instead of scattered ifs. Nested formats (brackets, \u003ccode>3[ab]\u003c/code>-style repeats) add a stack of partial results.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Run-length encoding\u003c/b> replaces each maximal run of a character by (character, count): \"aaabcc\" becomes a3 b1 c2. One pass, O(n). The look-and-say sequence is RLE applied to the previous term, read aloud.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why delimiters fail.\u003c/b> Join [\"a,b\", \"c\"] with \",\" and you get \"a,b,c\", which decodes to three values. Either escape the delimiter inside values (CSV doubles quotes; JSON escapes backslashes and quotes), or write each value's \u003cb>length\u003c/b> first. Real formats do the second: HTTP bodies carry Content-Length, Redis's protocol sends bulk strings as \u003ccode>$&lt;length&gt;\\r\\n&lt;data&gt;\\r\\n\u003c/code>, and Protocol Buffers prefix strings and nested messages with a varint length. A length prefix needs no scanning of the data, so it handles binary content.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import re\n\nTOKEN = re.compile(r\"\\s*(?:(?P&lt;num&gt;\\d+)|(?P&lt;name&gt;[A-Za-z_]\\w*)|(?P&lt;op&gt;\\S))\")\n\n\ndef tokens(text):\n    \"\"\"[(kind, value), ...] for numbers, names and single-character operators.\"\"\"\n    out = []\n    for m in TOKEN.finditer(text):\n        kind = m.lastgroup\n        if kind:                               # None only for trailing whitespace\n            out.append((kind, m.group(kind)))\n    return out\n\n\ndef rle(s):\n    \"\"\"'aaabcc' -&gt; [('a', 3), ('b', 1), ('c', 2)]\"\"\"\n    runs = []\n    for ch in s:\n        if runs and runs[-1][0] == ch:\n            runs[-1][1] += 1\n        else:\n            runs.append([ch, 1])\n    return [(ch, n) for ch, n in runs]\n\n\ndef encode(strings):\n    \"\"\"Length-prefixed: any characters, including ':' and digits, survive a round trip.\"\"\"\n    return \"\".join(f\"{len(s)}:{s}\" for s in strings)\n\n\ndef decode(data):\n    out, i = [], 0\n    while i &lt; len(data):\n        j = data.index(\":\", i)                 # the length field itself never contains ':'\n        n = int(data[i:j])\n        out.append(data[j + 1:j + 1 + n])\n        i = j + 1 + n\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>encode([\"a:b\", \"\", \"5:x\"])\u003c/code> is \"3:a:b0:3:5:x\". Decoding never looks inside a value, so the colons and digits in the data are harmless.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 0: the first \":\" after i is at 1, so the length is \"3\"; take the 3 characters after it: \"a:b\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>data  \u003cmark>3:\u003c/mark>\u003cmark>a:b\u003c/mark>0:3:5:x\nout   [\"a:b\"]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 5: \":\" at 6, length \"0\": an empty string, and the next field starts at 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>data  3:a:b\u003cmark>0:\u003c/mark>3:5:x\nout   [\"a:b\", \"\"]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 7: \":\" at 8, length \"3\": take \"5:x\". i = 12 = len(data): done\u003c/p>\u003cpre>data  3:a:b0:\u003cmark>3:\u003c/mark>\u003cmark>5:x\u003c/mark>\nout   [\"a:b\", \"\", \"5:x\"]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>split(\" \")\u003c/code> versus \u003ccode>split()\u003c/code>\u003c/b>: the first keeps empty strings between repeated spaces; the second collapses whitespace.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unclear states\u003c/b>: \"  +-12\" and \"-\" are where ad hoc parsers break. List the states and test every transition, including empty input and a lone sign.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overflow in other languages\u003c/b>: accumulate with a check before multiplying by 10 (Python's ints never overflow, so clamp explicitly when the problem asks for 32-bit results).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Trusting a length field\u003c/b> from untrusted input: check it against the remaining data before slicing, or a corrupted prefix silently swallows the next value.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does decoding \"3:a:b0:3:5:x\" never misread the \":\" inside \"a:b\" or \"5:x\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The decoder searches for \":\" only at the start of a field, to end the length; it then jumps over exactly n characters without inspecting them. Characters inside a value are never treated as structure.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to tokenise before parsing, model a parser as states, run-length encode, and encode lists so that any content round-trips.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:strings/find-the-duplicates-repeated-passages-across-doc":"\u003cp>Plagiarism checkers, deduplicating backup tools and near-duplicate detectors all ask the same question: which passages of length L appear in more than one document? You will answer it three ways on about 420,000 characters of synthetic documents with planted copies, check that all three agree exactly, then measure time and memory for L = 50, 500 and 2,000. The point is not only \"O(n) beats O(nL)\", but where that crossover really is in CPython, and what collisions cost.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10 or newer; no packages needed. The run takes about two minutes, most of it the memory measurements (tracemalloc slows allocation down).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>dup_lab.py\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>by_slices\u003c/code> is the exact baseline (every window as a dict key). The two hash-based methods store one integer per window and \u003cb>verify\u003c/b> every hit against the real text, so a collision can cost time but never a wrong answer.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\"\"\"Find passages of L characters that appear in two different documents: exact slices, hashed slices, rolling hash.\"\"\"\nimport random\nimport time\nimport tracemalloc\n\nMOD = (1 &lt;&lt; 61) - 1                      # Mersenne prime modulus\nBASE = random.randrange(256, MOD - 1)    # random base, chosen once per run\n\n\ndef make_docs(n_docs=30, words=2_000, plants=30, seed=7):\n    \"\"\"Random-word documents plus planted copies of 60-3000 character passages.\"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    vocab = [\"\".join(rng.choices(\"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\", k=rng.randint(2, 9))) for _ in range(5_000)]\n    docs = [\" \".join(rng.choices(vocab, k=words)) for _ in range(n_docs)]\n    for _ in range(plants):\n        a, b = rng.sample(range(n_docs), 2)\n        size = rng.choice((60, 300, 3_000))\n        start = rng.randrange(len(docs[a]) - size)\n        pos = rng.randrange(len(docs[b]))\n        docs[b] = docs[b][:pos] + docs[a][start:start + size] + docs[b][pos:]\n    return docs\n\n\ndef by_slices(docs, L):\n    \"\"\"Exact baseline: every window is a dict key. Returns the set of (first doc, later doc) pairs.\"\"\"\n    first, pairs = {}, set()\n    for d, text in enumerate(docs):\n        for i in range(len(text) - L + 1):\n            owner = first.setdefault(text[i:i + L], d)\n            if owner != d:\n                pairs.add((owner, d))\n    return pairs\n\n\ndef _check(buckets, docs, L, key, d, i, pairs):\n    \"\"\"A hash hit. buckets[key] holds the first (doc &lt;&lt; 32 | start) of each distinct window with this hash.\n    Compare the real text, so a collision costs time but never a wrong answer. Returns 1 for a collision.\"\"\"\n    window = docs[d][i:i + L]\n    entry = buckets[key]\n    entries = entry if isinstance(entry, list) else [entry]\n    for packed in entries:\n        od, oi = packed &gt;&gt; 32, packed &amp; 0xFFFFFFFF\n        if docs[od][oi:oi + L] == window:\n            if od != d:\n                pairs.add((od, d))\n            return 0\n    buckets[key] = entries + [d &lt;&lt; 32 | i]          # same hash, different text\n    return 1\n\n\ndef by_slice_hash(docs, L):\n    buckets, pairs, collisions = {}, set(), 0\n    for d, text in enumerate(docs):\n        for i in range(len(text) - L + 1):\n            key = hash(text[i:i + L])                   # O(L): slice, then hash, both in C\n            if key in buckets:\n                collisions += _check(buckets, docs, L, key, d, i, pairs)\n            else:\n                buckets[key] = d &lt;&lt; 32 | i\n    return pairs, collisions\n\n\ndef by_rolling(docs, L, mod=MOD):\n    buckets, pairs, collisions = {}, set(), 0\n    top = pow(BASE, L - 1, mod)\n    for d, text in enumerate(docs):\n        if len(text) &lt; L:\n            continue\n        codes = [ord(ch) for ch in text]\n        h = 0\n        for c in codes[:L]:\n            h = (h * BASE + c) % mod\n        for i in range(len(text) - L + 1):\n            if h in buckets:\n                collisions += _check(buckets, docs, L, h, d, i, pairs)\n            else:\n                buckets[h] = d &lt;&lt; 32 | i\n            if i + L &lt; len(text):\n                h = ((h - codes[i] * top) * BASE + codes[i + L]) % mod   # O(1) slide\n    return pairs, collisions\n\n\ndef timed(fn, *args):\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    out = fn(*args)\n    return out, time.perf_counter() - t\n\n\ndef peak_mb(fn, *args):\n    tracemalloc.start()\n    fn(*args)\n    peak = tracemalloc.get_traced_memory()[1] / 1e6\n    tracemalloc.stop()\n    return peak\n\n\ndef main():\n    docs = make_docs()\n    print(f\"{len(docs)} documents, {sum(map(len, docs)):,} characters\")\n    for L in (50, 500, 2_000):\n        print(f\"L = {L}\")\n        results = {}\n        methods = [(\"hash(slice)\", by_slice_hash, ()), (\"rolling hash\", by_rolling, ()),\n                   (\"rolling, mod 1000003\", by_rolling, (1_000_003,))]\n        if L &lt;= 500:\n            methods.insert(0, (\"dict of slices\", by_slices, ()))\n        for name, fn, extra in methods:\n            out, secs = timed(fn, docs, L, *extra)\n            results[name] = out if isinstance(out, set) else out[0]\n            note = \"\" if isinstance(out, set) else f\"  collisions {out[1]}\"\n            print(f\"  {name:21} {secs:6.2f} s  peak {peak_mb(fn, docs, L, *extra):6.1f} MB{note}\")\n        answers = list(results.values())\n        assert all(a == answers[0] for a in answers), \"methods disagree\"\n        print(f\"  all methods agree: {len(answers[0])} document pairs share a passage\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Predict before running: which method is fastest at L = 50, and at L = 2,000? Which uses the most memory at L = 500? Roughly how many collisions will the modulus 1,000,003 cause on about 420,000 windows?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>python3 dup_lab.py\u003c/code> and copy the output into a table: method by L, with time and peak memory.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Explain the L = 50 row. Slicing and hashing 50 characters runs in C and costs about as much as the handful of Python operations the rolling update needs per window, so O(nL) in C competes with O(n) in bytecode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Explain the memory column: \u003ccode>by_slices\u003c/code> keeps every window as a string of about 49 + L bytes, while the hash methods keep one integer per window whatever L is.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Explain the collision count with the birthday effect: with N windows and M buckets you expect about N^2 / (2M) colliding pairs while N is much smaller than M, and when N approaches M a large fraction of new windows land in an occupied bucket. Then confirm the answers still agree: verification removed every false hit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stretch: winnowing, the fingerprinting idea used by plagiarism detectors. Instead of storing every window hash, store only the smallest hash in each run of w consecutive window hashes. Implement it for w = 20 and check that every planted passage of at least L + w - 1 characters is still found while far fewer fingerprints are stored.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Times on a recent machine (a shared one can be twice as slow); memory is stable:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>L\u003c/th>\u003cth>dict of slices\u003c/th>\u003cth>hash(slice)\u003c/th>\u003cth>rolling hash\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>50\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.4 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.5 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.8-1.3 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.7 s, 220 MB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.5-0.7 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.8-1 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(skipped: about 0.9 GB)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 1-1.1 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.7-0.9 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>All methods agree: about 32 document pairs share a 50-character passage and about 10 share a 500- or 2,000-character one (only the long planted copies are that long).\u003c/li>\u003cli>At L = 50 the C-level slicing methods win; the rolling hash's cost is flat in L while slicing grows with it, so the rolling hash catches up somewhere between 500 and 2,000 characters and is ahead at 2,000. In a compiled language the crossover comes far earlier.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Exact slices are fine for short L and hopeless for long L: memory grows with L, about four times larger at 500 than at 50.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With the modulus 1,000,003, expect tens of thousands of collisions, a little extra time for verification, and exactly the same answer. With 2^61 - 1, zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A service must find repeated 4 KB blocks across terabytes of backups. Which approach from this lab scales, and what would you change about it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The rolling hash: its cost per byte does not depend on the block size, and it stores one small integer per candidate instead of a 4 KB key. In production you would write it in a compiled language, store only some fingerprints (content-defined boundaries as restic does, or winnowing), keep the hash index on disk, and confirm candidates with a strong hash or a byte comparison, as rsync does.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:205":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Decide whether each character of one string can be renamed consistently to obtain the other, with distinct source characters assigned distinct targets. Positions are significant: \u003ccode>egg\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>add\u003c/code> qualify, while \u003ccode>ab\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>cc\u003c/code> do not.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The strings must have equal lengths; this implementation returns false otherwise. Characters are arbitrary Python code points, so an array indexed only by lowercase letters would unnecessarily restrict the inputs. Empty strings satisfy the condition.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Decide whether the letters of one string can be renamed, consistently and one-to-one, to produce another string of the same length\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A renaming must send every copy of a letter to the same letter, and two different letters may not share a target. What pattern do both strings have to share?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counts and canonical shapes\u003c/p>\u003cp>Map each character to the index of its first occurrence; two strings are related by a one-to-one renaming exactly when these first-occurrence patterns are equal. Two dicts (s to t and t to s) checked position by position work too; checking only one direction is the classic bug.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(alphabet) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every pair of positions, compare whether the source characters are equal and whether the corresponding target characters are equal. The two equality results must agree for all pairs. This uses O(n²) time and O(1) auxiliary space but revisits the same character relationships repeatedly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep a mapping in each direction while scanning aligned positions. The forward map prevents one source from changing its target; the reverse map prevents two sources from merging. If a pair is compatible, record both directions. After each position, the maps represent a bijection on exactly the characters encountered so far. A conflicting pair cannot occur in any valid renaming, and completing the scan gives a consistent one-to-one mapping.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Compare e with a and g with d\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Record e -&gt; a, a -&gt; e, g -&gt; d, d -&gt; g\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Compare the final g with d\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Both existing mappings agree, so return \u003cmark>true\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def is_isomorphic(source, target):\n    if len(source) != len(target):\n        return False\n    forward, reverse = {}, {}\n    for a, b in zip(source, target):\n        if a in forward and forward[a] != b:\n            return False\n        if b in reverse and reverse[b] != a:\n            return False\n        forward[a] = b\n        reverse[b] = a\n    return True\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Expected O(n) time for dictionary operations and O(d) space, where d is the number of distinct characters encountered. The Python hash-table bounds are expected rather than worst-case guarantees.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>One direction alone incorrectly accepts \u003ccode>ab\u003c/code> mapped to \u003ccode>cc\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compare lengths before using \u003ccode>zip\u003c/code>, which otherwise silently truncates.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A mapping is between code points; normalization and grapheme clusters are separate choices.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compare the sequence of first-occurrence indices in each string instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Test whether a list of words has the same equality pattern as a pattern string.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:28":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the starting index of the first occurrence of a pattern in a text, or -1 if it is absent. Treat the empty pattern as occurring at index 0. For \u003ccode>text = \"ababa\"\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pattern = \"aba\"\u003c/code>, the result is 0.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Both strings may be empty and may contain arbitrary code points. Overlapping matches are possible, though only the earliest is requested. Repeated characters make naive restarting expensive; the scan should avoid moving the text index backward.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Return where a pattern first occurs inside a text, or -1\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>After a partial match fails, the naive method restarts from the next text position. Could the part you already matched tell you where the pattern can resume?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix function (KMP)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Build the prefix function of the pattern; scan the text once, and on a mismatch fall back to pi[k - 1] characters matched instead of zero, so the text pointer never moves backwards. The first time k reaches m, the answer is i - m + 1. (In everyday Python, text.find(pattern) is the right tool.)\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + m) time, O(m) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At each legal starting position, compare the pattern against the corresponding text characters until a mismatch or a full match. With text length n and pattern length m, worst-case time is O(nm), as almost all comparisons can succeed before each mismatch. Comparing characters directly needs O(1) auxiliary space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each pattern prefix, compute the length of its longest proper prefix that is also a suffix. When a comparison fails after k matched characters, any possible continuation must begin with a border of those k characters. Falling back to the longest such border preserves every viable candidate; further mismatches follow its own border links. The text index never retreats, and total fallback steps are amortized linear because k can increase only once per scanned character.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">pattern = aba, prefix lengths = [0, 0, 1]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Scan a then b: matched length becomes 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Scan a\u003c/p>\u003cpre>matched length becomes 3; return 2 - 3 + 1 = \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def find_first(text, pattern):\n    if not pattern:\n        return 0\n    pi = [0] * len(pattern)\n    k = 0\n    for i in range(1, len(pattern)):\n        while k and pattern[i] != pattern[k]:\n            k = pi[k - 1]\n        if pattern[i] == pattern[k]:\n            k += 1\n        pi[i] = k\n    k = 0\n    for i, char in enumerate(text):\n        while k and char != pattern[k]:\n            k = pi[k - 1]\n        if char == pattern[k]:\n            k += 1\n        if k == len(pattern):\n            return i - len(pattern) + 1\n    return -1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The prefix table costs O(m) time and space. The text scan costs O(n), so total time is O(n + m) and auxiliary space is O(m). In ordinary Python, \u003ccode>text.find(pattern)\u003c/code> is usually simpler.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>pi[k - 1]\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>pi[k]\u003c/code>, when falling back.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Handle the empty pattern before indexing it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return as soon as the first full match ends, including when it ends at the final text character.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Report all occurrences by falling back after a full match instead of returning.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a prefix automaton for repeated scans over a fixed small alphabet.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:459":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Determine whether a nonempty string consists of at least two copies of a shorter nonempty string. For example, \u003ccode>abcabc\u003c/code> qualifies, but \u003ccode>abca\u003c/code> does not. This implementation also returns false for an empty input.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The repeating unit must cover the entire input; a repeated prefix with a leftover tail is insufficient. A one-character string cannot be formed from two nonempty copies. Arbitrary code points are supported.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Can the whole string be produced by writing some shorter substring several times in a row?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If the string is a repetition of a block, what is true of its longest proper prefix that is also a suffix?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix function (KMP)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Let b = pi[n - 1] be the longest border; then p = n - b is the smallest period, and the string is a whole repetition exactly when b &gt; 0 and n % p == 0. Equivalently, s occurs in (s + s)[1:-1]: rotating a repetition by one period gives the string back.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try each possible unit length from 1 through half the input length. When the length divides n, repeat the corresponding prefix and compare it with the original. Constructing and checking each candidate can cost O(n), for O(n²) worst-case time and O(n) space for a constructed candidate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The prefix function gives the longest proper border length b, where a border is both a prefix and suffix. Consequently p = n - b is the shortest period: the border equality enforces \u003ccode>s[i] == s[i - p]\u003c/code> wherever both indices exist. Conversely any period q gives a border of length n - q, so no shorter period exists. The string is a whole repetition exactly when b is positive and p divides n. Divisibility is essential: a periodic string can stop partway through its next block.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">For abcabc, the longest proper border is abc\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 6, b = 3, so shortest period p = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">6 is divisible by 3 and b is positive\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>true\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def repeated_substring(s):\n    if not s:\n        return False\n    pi = [0] * len(s)\n    k = 0\n    for i in range(1, len(s)):\n        while k and s[i] != s[k]:\n            k = pi[k - 1]\n        if s[i] == s[k]:\n            k += 1\n        pi[i] = k\n    border = pi[-1]\n    return border &gt; 0 and len(s) % (len(s) - border) == 0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Computing the prefix table takes O(n) amortized time and O(n) auxiliary space. Each successful extension raises k by one, and each fallback lowers it, bounding the total number of fallback steps.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A border alone does not ensure a whole number of repetitions: \u003ccode>ababa\u003c/code> has period 2 but length 5.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The entire string is not a shorter unit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Avoid accessing the last prefix entry for an empty string.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the shortest repeating unit rather than a Boolean.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find every valid unit length by following the border chain and testing divisibility.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:5":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return a longest contiguous substring that reads identically from left to right and right to left. If several have maximum length, any one is acceptable. For \u003ccode>babad\u003c/code>, either \u003ccode>bab\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>aba\u003c/code> is valid. An empty input returns an empty string.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The substring must be contiguous, not a subsequence obtained by skipping characters. Both odd and even lengths are possible. Python strings are sequences of code points; this algorithm compares those code points directly and does not perform Unicode normalization. An answer must be materialized as a new string, but candidate slices need not be created.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Return the longest contiguous piece of the string that reads the same in both directions\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every such piece is symmetric about a middle point. How many possible middles are there, and what can you do from each?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Expand around centres\u003c/p>\u003cp>Expand from each of the 2n - 1 centres (each character and each gap) while the two ends match; the widest span found wins. Each centre costs its radius, so O(n^2) in the worst case with O(1) extra space (Manacher brings it to O(n)).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^2) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate each pair of endpoints and check the corresponding substring by comparing inward. There are O(n²) candidates and a check can take O(n), giving O(n³) time in the worst case. Direct index comparisons need O(1) workspace; slicing every candidate would add allocation and copying costs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every nonempty palindrome has exactly one center, either a character or the gap between adjacent characters. Starting at that center, matching outward endpoints yields all palindromes with that center in increasing size. Once a pair mismatches, every larger candidate with the same center includes that mismatch, so further expansion cannot succeed. Visiting all centers therefore examines every possible palindrome; keep only the best half-open interval. Length-one centers ensure that a nonempty input always has a candidate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">s = cbbd; try the gap between the two b characters\u003c/p>\u003cpre>b equals b, so the interval [1, 3) is a palindrome\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Expand to c and d\u003c/p>\u003cpre>They differ; keep [1, 3) and return \u003cmark>bb\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The interval is updated only when it is strictly longer than the current best. This deliberately leaves ties with the earlier discovered candidate; the problem does not require a particular tie choice.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_palindrome(s):\n    best_left = best_right = 0\n    for center in range(len(s)):\n        for left, right in ((center, center), (center, center + 1)):\n            while left &gt;= 0 and right &lt; len(s) and s[left] == s[right]:\n                if right - left + 1 &gt; best_right - best_left:\n                    best_left, best_right = left, right + 1\n                left -= 1\n                right += 1\n    return s[best_left:best_right]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(n) centers and each can expand O(n) times, giving O(n²) worst-case time. Auxiliary workspace is O(1), excluding the returned substring, which can occupy O(n) space. On strings with little symmetry, expansions often stop quickly, but that is not a worst-case guarantee.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Forgetting gap centers misses \u003ccode>bb\u003c/code> and other even palindromes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not return the interval after the failed comparison without correcting its boundaries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Track indices instead of repeatedly slicing, which can introduce substantial copying.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A single character, an empty string, and a string of identical characters deserve separate tests.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count all palindromic substrings by adding one per successful expansion.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use Manacher's algorithm to compute maximum radii in O(n) time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return all distinct longest values, taking care to define how duplicates are handled.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:647":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the nonempty contiguous substrings that are palindromes. Occurrences at different positions count separately even when their text is the same. For \u003ccode>aaa\u003c/code>, the answer is 6: three length-one occurrences, two length-two occurrences, and one length-three occurrence.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every center may contribute several lengths, and both character centers and gap centers matter. The input may be empty, in which case there are no nonempty substrings. The result can grow quadratically in n, so fixed-width implementations must choose an appropriately wide counter. Python integers grow as necessary.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count how many contiguous substrings read the same in both directions, counting equal substrings at different positions separately\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Use the same middles as for the longest one. How many palindromes does one successful expansion step add?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Expand around centres\u003c/p>\u003cp>Expand around all 2n - 1 centres; every step where the ends still match is exactly one more palindrome centred there, so the answer is the total number of successful steps (a maximal span of length L contributes (L + 1) // 2).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^2) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all O(n²) endpoint pairs and check each candidate by comparing the characters from its ends inward. One test can take O(n), yielding O(n³) worst-case time and O(1) auxiliary space if the check uses indices. The repeated checks recompute the inner palindrome for longer candidates.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A palindrome is determined by its center and its radius. Begin at each character and each gap, and expand while both endpoints are in range and equal. Every successful comparison creates exactly one palindrome, since the previously tested inner interval is already known to be symmetric. Once a mismatch or boundary stops the expansion, no larger palindrome at that center can exist. Every palindrome has one unique center, so summing successful steps neither misses nor double-counts occurrences.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">s = aaa; the three character centers contribute 1, 2, and 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Their total is 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The two gaps each contribute one length-two palindrome\u003c/p>\u003cpre>total = 4 + 1 + 1 = \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The two copies of \u003ccode>aa\u003c/code> are counted separately because they have different gap centers. A successful expansion counts an occurrence, not merely a distinct string value.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For an even center, the initial empty interior is symmetric. Matching the two adjacent characters creates the first nonempty palindrome, and each later step adds one character to each side.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_palindromes(s):\n    count = 0\n    for center in range(len(s)):\n        for left, right in ((center, center), (center, center + 1)):\n            while left &gt;= 0 and right &lt; len(s) and s[left] == s[right]:\n                count += 1\n                left -= 1\n                right += 1\n    return count\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(n) centers and at most O(n) successful expansions per center, so time is O(n²) in the worst case. The algorithm uses O(1) auxiliary space. The final extra gap after the last character fails immediately and does not affect the count or asymptotic cost.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Counting only maximal palindromes loses the smaller ones at the same center.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Counting distinct contents instead of positions changes the requested result.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Include odd and even centers, and return zero for empty input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For n identical characters the expected answer is n(n + 1)/2.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Record the longest interval instead of counting successful expansions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use Manacher radii to sum contributions in O(n) time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count distinct palindrome values with a palindromic tree.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:151":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a sentence whose words are separated by spaces, return the words in reverse order, separated by one space, with no leading or trailing space. For \u003ccode>  red   blue \u003c/code>, return \u003ccode>blue red\u003c/code>. All-space input produces an empty string.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>This version treats the literal ASCII space as the separator and preserves all other characters inside words, including tabs. It accepts an empty string. Python strings are immutable, so the returned text and token references require storage; the implementation does not claim an in-place transformation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Reverse the order of the words in a sentence, removing leading, trailing and repeated spaces\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Treat the sentence as a list of tokens rather than characters. Which split collapses runs of spaces for you?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Tokenisers, state machines and encodings\u003c/p>\u003cp>Tokenise with split() (no argument), which drops empty strings, then join the reversed list with single spaces. Without a split, reverse the whole character buffer, then reverse each word and compact spaces with a write index.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space (O(1) extra for the in-place buffer version)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Repeatedly scan the remaining prefix from the beginning to find its final word, append that word to the answer, then shrink the prefix. Repeating a linear scan for each of O(n) short words can take O(n²) time. Building the answer by repeated concatenation can add further copying; at least O(n) space is required for the returned string.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Split once on the exact separator, discard empty tokens produced by consecutive or boundary spaces, reverse the remaining sequence, and join with a single separator. Every nonempty token is exactly one maximal run of non-space characters. Reversal changes only token order, while joining inserts exactly one space between neighboring output words. Thus neither the characters within a word nor their order are altered, and no extra separator can remain at either end.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Split \"  red   blue \" on spaces and discard empty tokens\u003c/p>\u003cpre>words = [red, blue]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse the tokens and join with one space\u003c/p>\u003cpre>result = \u003cmark>blue red\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Using \u003ccode>split(\" \")\u003c/code> rather than the no-argument form is intentional: no-argument splitting also treats tabs, newlines and other whitespace as separators. Either convention can be useful, but they are different contracts.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For instance, a tab inside \u003ccode>red\u003c/code> followed by \u003ccode>blue\u003c/code> is part of one word under this contract, rather than a boundary between two words.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def reverse_words(sentence):\n    words = [word for word in sentence.split(\" \") if word]\n    words.reverse()\n    return \" \".join(words)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Splitting, filtering, reversing and joining together take O(n) time. The tokens, list, and returned string use O(n) space. Empty tokens from repeated spaces are also bounded by input length.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reversing the entire character string alone reverses each word as well.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Joining unfiltered tokens preserves unwanted repeated spaces.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Decide explicitly whether tabs and other whitespace are separators.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One word should be returned unchanged except for surrounding spaces.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Treat all Unicode whitespace as separators with \u003ccode>split()\u003c/code> instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Given a mutable character buffer, compact spaces and reverse individual words after reversing the whole buffer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Preserve the original spacing and reverse only the word sequence; specify how separators are assigned.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:8":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Read an optional sign followed by consecutive ASCII decimal digits after skipping leading ASCII spaces. Stop at the first other character, return zero if there are no digits, and clamp the value to the signed 32-bit interval. For \u003ccode>  -42rest\u003c/code>, return -42.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The parser consumes only a prefix, not every number embedded in the string. A sign is allowed once and only before digits; a space after the sign ends the digit run. Limits are -2³¹ and 2³¹ - 1, which differ by one in magnitude. Very long inputs should not require constructing an enormous Python integer.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Read a signed integer from the front of a messy string and clamp it to the 32-bit range\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Write down the stages the input goes through (spaces, an optional sign, digits) and what ends each one.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Tokenisers, state machines and encodings\u003c/p>\u003cp>A small state machine: skip leading spaces, read at most one sign, then accumulate digits until the first non-digit; clamp the result to [-2^31, 2^31 - 1]. In languages with fixed-width ints, check before multiplying by 10.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One could identify the digit substring, convert it to an arbitrary-precision integer, apply the sign, then clamp. This takes at least O(n) scanning time and O(n) substring space; very long integers also have conversion and arithmetic costs, and Python may reject conversions past its configured digit limit. An incremental bounded accumulator avoids those problems.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The parser has three ordered phases: skip spaces, consume at most one sign, then consume digits. Accumulate a nonnegative magnitude with limit 2³¹ for negative numbers and 2³¹ - 1 otherwise. Before the next multiply and add, compare the existing magnitude with the largest safe predecessor. If that digit would cross the limit, the final clamped result is already determined, because remaining digits can only increase the magnitude. If no overflow happens, the accumulated magnitude is exactly the value of the consumed prefix by the usual base-ten recurrence.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input \"  -42rest\": skip two spaces and read the minus sign\u003c/p>\u003cpre>limit = 2147483648, magnitude = 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read 4 then 2; r stops the digit phase\u003c/p>\u003cpre>signed magnitude is \u003cmark>-42\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The comparison against the limit happens before multiplication, so the same logic works in a fixed-width language when the limits and intermediate operations are represented safely.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def parse_int32(text):\n    i = 0\n    while i &lt; len(text) and text[i] == \" \":\n        i += 1\n    sign = 1\n    if i &lt; len(text) and text[i] in \"+-\":\n        if text[i] == \"-\":\n            sign = -1\n        i += 1\n    limit = (1 &lt;&lt; 31) if sign &lt; 0 else (1 &lt;&lt; 31) - 1\n    value = 0\n    while i &lt; len(text) and \"0\" &lt;= text[i] &lt;= \"9\":\n        digit = ord(text[i]) - ord(\"0\")\n        if value &gt; (limit - digit) // 10:\n            return -limit if sign &lt; 0 else limit\n        value = value * 10 + digit\n        i += 1\n    return sign * value\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The scan visits at most n characters and maintains a bounded accumulator: O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space. It may return early as soon as overflow is unavoidable.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>isdigit()\u003c/code> recognizes non-ASCII digits that are outside this contract.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>+-2\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>- 2\u003c/code>, an empty input, and a sign with no digits all yield zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Clamp the negative endpoint separately; its magnitude is one larger.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A leading tab is not an ASCII space in this contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return both the value and the index where parsing stopped.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reject overflow instead of saturating, or support a different base.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Recognize a broader whitespace set only if the input contract calls for it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:38":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start with the string \u003ccode>1\u003c/code>. Each subsequent term describes the preceding term as consecutive runs: write the decimal run length followed by the run's character, for each run in order. Return the nth term for a positive integer n. The first four terms are \u003ccode>1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>11\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>21\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>1211\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The operation describes runs, not the total frequency of each character. Runs with the same character separated by another character remain separate. The number of terms is positive; this implementation rejects nonpositive n. Intermediate terms can grow, so avoid repeated string concatenation inside the run loop.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each term of a sequence describes the previous term by reading out its groups of equal digits\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>How do you describe \"111221\" in one left-to-right pass? What counts as one group?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Tokenisers, state machines and encodings\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each term is the run-length encoding of the previous one, written as count then digit. Scan maximal runs, append str(count) and the digit to a list, join once, and repeat n - 1 times; the terms grow by roughly 30% each step, so build with join.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(total length of all terms generated) time\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One inefficient method finds a run by comparing each position against all earlier positions in that run, then constructs the next term by appending fragments one at a time to an immutable string. Repeated comparisons or copies can make one transformation quadratic in its input and output lengths. It also obscures the exact boundary between adjacent groups.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Scan a term from left to right. At index i, advance j until it reaches the first different character or the end. The interval [i, j) is a maximal run, and its description is \u003ccode>str(j - i)\u003c/code> followed by \u003ccode>term[i]\u003c/code>. Append these pieces to a list and continue from j; a single join builds the new term. The intervals are nonempty, disjoint, and cover the preceding term in order, so each run is described exactly once. Repeating this transformation n - 1 times establishes the requested term by induction.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">term = 21: the first maximal run contains one 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>emit 12\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The next maximal run contains one 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>emit 11; join to obtain \u003cmark>1211\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A run count is written in decimal and may require multiple digits for a general input to this encoding operation, even though the generated sequence starts from \u003ccode>1\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_and_say(n):\n    if n &lt; 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be positive\")\n    term = \"1\"\n    for _ in range(n - 1):\n        pieces = []\n        i = 0\n        while i &lt; len(term):\n            j = i + 1\n            while j &lt; len(term) and term[j] == term[i]:\n                j += 1\n            pieces.extend((str(j - i), term[i]))\n            i = j\n        term = \"\".join(pieces)\n    return term\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let L be the sum of the lengths of all generated terms including the answer. Scanning and emitting each run costs O(L) overall, with O(maximum term length) working space for the current term and the next term's pieces. This output-sensitive bound avoids assuming a particular growth rate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Start with \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> at n = 1; do not apply the transformation n times.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Adjacent equal characters are one run, but separated equal characters are not.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Flush the final run even when it reaches the end of the string.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Joining fragments once avoids repeated whole-prefix copying.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Implement a generic run-length encoder for an arbitrary string.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stream each term's pieces to a consumer rather than returning a single string.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Specify a delimiter or escaping scheme if encoded counts and symbols must later be decoded unambiguously.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1392":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the longest nonempty proper prefix of a string that is also a suffix. Proper means the prefix cannot be the whole input, although the prefix and suffix are allowed to overlap. Return the empty string if no such border exists. For \u003ccode>level\u003c/code>, the answer is \u003ccode>l\u003c/code>; for \u003ccode>aaaa\u003c/code>, it is \u003ccode>aaa\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The answer is a contiguous prefix and a contiguous suffix, not an arbitrary matching subsequence. An input may be empty or contain arbitrary Python code points. Overlap is permitted, so an algorithm that caps the border length at half the string is incorrect. A long run of the same character creates many candidate borders and exposes repeated comparison costs. The result is a slice, so its allocation must be counted separately from the table used to find it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Return the longest non-empty prefix of a string that is also a suffix, without being the whole string\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>This is precisely one value that the KMP preprocessing computes. Which one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix function (KMP)\u003c/p>\u003cp>The answer is s[:pi[n - 1]], the longest border. Alternatively compare prefix and suffix hashes for every length from longest to shortest and verify the first equal pair.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Test candidate lengths from n - 1 down to 1, comparing the prefix and suffix character by character. The first successful candidate is the longest because candidates are considered in decreasing order. Each comparison can take O(n), and there are O(n) candidates, giving O(n²) worst-case time. Index-based comparisons use O(1) auxiliary space, apart from the returned slice; making slices at every step allocates extra intermediate strings.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build the prefix function pi, where pi[i] is the length of the longest proper border of the prefix ending at i. Suppose a prefix ending just before i has a border of length k. To extend that border, compare the next character with the character at position k. If they differ, a shorter candidate must itself be a border of the length-k prefix: any suffix that could extend to a border also ends that prefix. The next candidate is therefore pi[k - 1]. Repeating this fallback tries all relevant candidates in decreasing order without revisiting arbitrary positions.\u003c/p>\u003cp>When the characters match, adding one gives the longest extendable candidate; if none match, the answer for this position is zero. This maintains the definition of pi by induction. At the final position, pi[-1] is exactly the longest proper border requested. Its length is less than n because pi only represents proper borders. The algorithm does not forbid the two occurrences from overlapping.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">s = aaaa: process the second and third characters\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pi becomes [0, 1, 2, 0] so far\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The final a extends the border of length 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pi[3] = 3; return s[:3] = \u003cmark>aaa\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In the fallback case, the invariant is that k is a border length of the already processed prefix. Reducing it through the table preserves this invariant, while discarding lengths that cannot possibly match the current character. Over a complete scan, increases of k total at most n and each fallback strictly decreases k; hence all fallback iterations together are linear.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The answer may be empty even when characters recur internally: both ends must match in the same order. For \u003ccode>abcabx\u003c/code>, a repeated internal prefix is not a suffix, so the final border length is zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_border(s):\n    if not s:\n        return \"\"\n    pi = [0] * len(s)\n    k = 0\n    for i in range(1, len(s)):\n        while k and s[i] != s[k]:\n            k = pi[k - 1]\n        if s[i] == s[k]:\n            k += 1\n        pi[i] = k\n    return s[:pi[-1]]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The prefix table takes O(n) time and O(n) auxiliary space. A returned border can contain O(n) characters, so the output itself can also occupy O(n) space. Each comparison and indexing operation is constant time under the normal Python string cost model.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Returning the whole string violates the proper-prefix requirement.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Restricting the two occurrences to disjoint intervals incorrectly rejects overlapping borders.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A mismatch needs \u003ccode>pi[k - 1]\u003c/code>, not a reset to zero or an access to \u003ccode>pi[k]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The empty string needs special treatment before accessing the last table entry.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Slicing a zero-length prefix correctly gives the empty result.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Follow the border chain from pi[-1] to enumerate all proper border lengths.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Test if the smallest period tiles the whole string by checking divisibility.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Search for a pattern in a text using the same fallback table.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:214":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Add characters only to the front of a string so that the result is a palindrome, using as few added characters as possible. Return the resulting string. For \u003ccode>aacecaaa\u003c/code>, one optimal result is \u003ccode>aaacecaaa\u003c/code>; an empty string needs no additions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The existing string must remain intact as a suffix of the result. A palindrome that could be formed by inserting characters elsewhere does not satisfy this operation. The input may contain any Python code point, including punctuation ordinarily used as a separator in string algorithms. The answer can be nearly twice the input length, so the output itself may require O(n) space.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Make the string a palindrome by adding as few characters as possible, only at the front\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Whatever you add in front mirrors part of the end. Which part of the original string can stay unmatched in the middle?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix function (KMP)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Find the longest palindromic prefix of s: it is the last prefix-function value of s + \"#\" + reversed(s), since a border there is a prefix of s equal to a suffix of its reverse. Answer: reversed(s[L:]) + s.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Check prefixes from longest to shortest, testing each by comparing from its ends toward the middle. Once a palindromic prefix is found, reverse the remaining suffix and prepend it. Up to O(n) prefix checks each cost O(n), yielding O(n²) worst-case time and O(1) workspace for index comparisons, excluding the result. Testing only prefixes is sufficient because the part that remains unpaired with the newly prepended characters must be a palindromic prefix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let L be the length of the longest palindromic prefix. The suffix after that prefix must be mirrored in front, giving \u003ccode>reverse(s[L:]) + s\u003c/code>. This construction is a palindrome because its outside pieces mirror each other and its middle is a palindrome. It adds n - L characters.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For optimality, suppose k characters are added. If k is less than n, the original prefix of length n - k lies in the central region of the finished palindrome, so it must itself be palindromic. Hence n - k cannot exceed L, and k is at least n - L. If k is at least n, it is certainly no smaller than n - L. Our construction reaches the lower bound.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To find L, compute the prefix function on the sequence \u003ccode>s\u003c/code>, a unique sentinel, and \u003ccode>reverse(s)\u003c/code>. A proper border cannot include the sentinel: there is only one, and it would have to occur at two distinct aligned positions. Therefore the final border lies completely inside the two string portions. A prefix of s equal to a suffix of its reverse is exactly a palindromic prefix. The longest border thus gives L. A fresh object supplies a sentinel unequal to every string character, rather than assuming an unused character exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">s = abcd; compare prefixes of abcd with suffixes of dcba\u003c/p>\u003cpre>the longest matching border has length 1, representing a\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse the remaining suffix bcd and prepend it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>dcb + abcd = \u003cmark>dcbabcd\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The sentinel is part of the prefix-function sequence, not part of the answer. The combined sequence is a list of characters and one object, so it remains safe even when the input contains \u003ccode>#\u003c/code>, a zero byte, or every ordinary punctuation character.\u003c/p>\u003cp>When the original string is already a palindrome, the border spans all n original characters without including the sentinel. The unmatched suffix is then empty, so the construction returns the original unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def shortest_front_palindrome(s):\n    marker = object()\n    seq = list(s) + [marker] + list(reversed(s))\n    pi = [0] * len(seq)\n    k = 0\n    for i in range(1, len(seq)):\n        while k and seq[i] != seq[k]:\n            k = pi[k - 1]\n        if seq[i] == seq[k]:\n            k += 1\n        pi[i] = k\n    prefix_len = pi[-1]\n    return s[prefix_len:][::-1] + s\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The combined sequence has 2n + 1 entries. Prefix preprocessing takes O(n) time by amortized fallback, and building the reversed suffix and result also takes O(n) time. Lists, table, and result require O(n) space. The empty input produces a one-element sentinel sequence and returns the empty string.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A literal separator can occur in the input and make the border cross the intended boundary.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The longest palindromic substring is not necessarily a prefix and cannot replace L.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mirror the suffix after the prefix, not the prefix itself.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Already-palindromic inputs need zero added characters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Test repeated characters, one-character inputs, empty input, and punctuation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Append only at the end by applying the same idea to the reversed string.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the number of characters needed rather than the resulting string.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find all palindromic prefix lengths by following the final border chain.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1044":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return a longest substring that occurs at least twice in a string. The occurrences may overlap; if several answers have the same maximum length, any is acceptable. Return the empty string if there is no repeated nonempty substring. For \u003ccode>banana\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ana\u003c/code> occurs at positions 1 and 3.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Inputs can be long, around 30,000 characters, so enumerating every substring and comparing every pair is impractical. Overlaps must not be excluded. Exact answers are required: a hash collision cannot be accepted as evidence that two windows match. This implementation uses Unicode code-point ordering and a deterministic suffix-array construction; it does not depend on a small alphabet or a probabilistic hash.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Find the longest substring that occurs at least twice (occurrences may overlap), for a string of up to 3 * 10^4 characters\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If some length works, every shorter length works too. How fast can you test one length?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Rolling hash\u003c/p>\u003cp>Binary search on the length L; test a length by sliding a polynomial hash over every window and checking for a repeat in a set, verifying candidates against the real text (or using a 61-bit modulus with a random base). Each test is O(n), so O(n log n) overall.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) expected time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every pair of starting positions, compare characters until they differ or one suffix ends, retaining the longest common prefix. There are O(n²) pairs and each comparison may inspect O(n) characters, for O(n³) worst-case time with O(1) auxiliary workspace apart from the output. For example, a string of identical characters makes many comparisons very long.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort all suffixes lexicographically. If two suffixes share a prefix x, every suffix between them in sorted order also begins with x: strings beginning with x form one contiguous lexicographic interval. Consequently the maximum common prefix of any pair is attained by some adjacent pair. The desired repeated substring is precisely such a common prefix, because every repeated substring begins two suffixes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Construct the suffix order by doubling. Initially rank each suffix by its first character. If ranks distinguish prefixes of length k, sort suffix starts by the pair of ranks for the first k characters and the next k characters. An out-of-range second half receives rank zero, smaller than any real rank. Equal pairs receive equal new ranks, and unequal pairs are ranked in sorted order; therefore the new ranks describe prefixes of length 2k. Once all ranks differ, the sorted order is the complete suffix order.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The ranks are consecutive integers, so each round can sort pairs in linear time with two stable counting sorts: first by the second rank, then by the first. Stability preserves the second-key order among entries with the same first key. This avoids an additional comparison-sort logarithm in every doubling round.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Next compute adjacent common-prefix lengths in linear time. Scan starts in text order, comparing each suffix to the preceding suffix in sorted order. If the preceding comparison matched h characters, dropping the first character leaves at least h - 1 matching characters with some earlier suffix. The immediate lexicographic predecessor shares at least that much, so reuse h - 1 before comparing further. The height falls by at most one per iteration and grows only when characters are actually compared, making the total growth linear.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sort the suffixes of banana\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a, ana, anana, banana, na, nana\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Adjacent ana and anana share three leading characters\u003c/p>\u003cpre>the maximum adjacent common prefix is \u003cmark>ana\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The two \u003ccode>ana\u003c/code> occurrences overlap, which is allowed. The algorithm stores their starting positions rather than requiring disjoint windows. The sentinel rank is numeric and is used only for absent suffix halves; no input character is reserved as a separator.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_duplicate(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    if n &lt; 2:\n        return \"\"\n    suffixes = sorted(range(n), key=s.__getitem__)\n    rank = [0] * n\n    classes = 0\n    for j, start in enumerate(suffixes):\n        if j == 0 or s[start] != s[suffixes[j - 1]]:\n            classes += 1\n        rank[start] = classes\n\n    def counting_sort(indices, keys, maximum):\n        counts = [0] * (maximum + 1)\n        for i in indices:\n            counts[keys[i]] += 1\n        total = 0\n        for key in range(len(counts)):\n            count = counts[key]\n            counts[key] = total\n            total += count\n        output = [0] * len(indices)\n        for i in indices:\n            key = keys[i]\n            output[counts[key]] = i\n            counts[key] += 1\n        return output\n\n    width = 1\n    while width &lt; n:\n        second = [rank[i + width] if i + width &lt; n else 0 for i in range(n)]\n        suffixes = counting_sort(suffixes, second, classes)\n        suffixes = counting_sort(suffixes, rank, classes)\n        new_rank = [0] * n\n        new_rank[suffixes[0]] = 1\n        for j in range(1, n):\n            a, b = suffixes[j - 1], suffixes[j]\n            a_key = (rank[a], second[a])\n            b_key = (rank[b], second[b])\n            new_rank[b] = new_rank[a] + (a_key != b_key)\n        rank = new_rank\n        classes = rank[suffixes[-1]]\n        if classes == n:\n            break\n        width *= 2\n    position = [0] * n\n    for j, start in enumerate(suffixes):\n        position[start] = j\n    height = best = best_start = 0\n    for start in range(n):\n        j = position[start]\n        if j == 0:\n            height = 0\n            continue\n        other = suffixes[j - 1]\n        while start + height &lt; n and other + height &lt; n and s[start + height] == s[other + height]:\n            height += 1\n        if height &gt; best:\n            best, best_start = height, start\n        if height:\n            height -= 1\n    return s[best_start:best_start + best]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(log n) doubling rounds. Each pair of counting sorts and rank assignment takes O(n) time because there are at most n ranks. Sorting the starting positions by their first characters initially takes O(n log n) comparison time, and the adjacent-prefix pass is O(n), for O(n log n) total time. Arrays and counting buckets occupy O(n) auxiliary space, plus O(n) for the returned slice. This deterministic bound avoids collision-based correctness qualifications.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Do not require occurrences to be disjoint; \u003ccode>aaaa\u003c/code> has answer \u003ccode>aaa\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sorting suffix strings directly creates O(n²) total copied characters in Python.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Give a missing second half a rank below every real rank.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reset the reused prefix height when there is no predecessor.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check empty, one-character, all-distinct, and all-equal inputs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use binary search and rolling hashes, verifying every hash collision against the original characters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build a suffix automaton and track occurrence counts to locate a longest repeated substring.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Restrict matches to nonoverlapping positions, which changes the feasibility condition.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2223":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each suffix of a string, measure how many leading characters it shares with the whole string. Return the sum of those lengths, including the whole string's contribution. For \u003ccode>ababa\u003c/code>, the contributions are 5, 0, 3, 0, and 1, totaling 9.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Suffixes are identified by starting positions, so repeated suffix-prefix matches contribute separately. The empty input has sum zero. A string of n identical characters has sum n(n + 1)/2; fixed-width implementations should therefore use a sufficiently wide result. Each character is compared as a Python code point, with no case folding or normalization.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Sum, over every suffix, the length of its longest common prefix with the whole string\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each term compares the string with itself shifted by i. Which array stores exactly those lengths?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Z-function\u003c/p>\u003cp>Those terms are the Z-function: z[i] is the longest common prefix of s and s[i:]. Compute it in O(n) with the Z-box; the answer is n (for the whole string) plus the sum of z[i] for i &gt;= 1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every start i, compare \u003ccode>s[0]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>s[i]\u003c/code>, then advance both positions until a mismatch or the end. On a run of identical characters this performs n + (n - 1) + ... + 1 comparisons, so time is O(n²). It needs O(1) auxiliary space if it uses indices rather than materializing suffixes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The Z-array stores exactly the needed values for positions i &gt; 0: z[i] is the length of the common prefix of s and s[i:]. The contribution at i = 0 is n. Maintain a half-open interval [left, right) that matches the prefix and has the furthest right endpoint found so far. If i lies inside this interval, the earlier Z-value at i - left supplies a known match, clipped to right - i. Beyond right, or when i is outside the box, compare explicitly.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The clipped reuse is safe because each position in the box equals the corresponding prefix position. When the mirrored Z-value ends strictly before the box boundary, its next mismatch is also a mismatch at i. When it reaches the boundary, the next characters have not yet been certified, so explicit comparisons extend the match. Update the box if the new match ends later. This yields the exact z[i] for each position by induction.\u003c/p>\u003cp>All successful comparisons beyond a current box extend the rightmost boundary, which never moves backward. At most one failed comparison ends each position's expansion. Along with constant work per position, this proves linear total time even on highly repetitive strings.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">s = ababa: the whole suffix contributes 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>z[1] = 0, z[2] = 3 for aba\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The remaining Z values are 0 and 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>5 + 0 + 3 + 0 + 1 = \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>At position 4, the known box from position 2 covers the final character, allowing the algorithm to reuse a length-one match. The half-open boundary makes the available reuse length \u003ccode>right - i\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>right - i + 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Consider a different box where the mirrored value is shorter than the remaining box: its mismatch is already inside the region known to equal the prefix. That mismatch proves the current value cannot extend, so reuse does not silently skip a better match. If the mirrored match fills the remainder, however, the character after the boundary has not been compared yet. The explicit loop tests it and may grow the box. This distinction prevents a common error: copying a mirrored Z-value without considering how much of it is certified by the current box.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def sum_suffix_scores(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    z = [0] * n\n    left = right = 0\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if i &lt; right:\n            z[i] = min(right - i, z[i - left])\n        while i + z[i] &lt; n and s[z[i]] == s[i + z[i]]:\n            z[i] += 1\n        if i + z[i] &gt; right:\n            left, right = i, i + z[i]\n    return n + sum(z)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The Z-array is built in O(n) time and stored in O(n) auxiliary space. Summing it is another O(n) pass. For n = 0, the loop and sum are empty and the result is zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Do not forget the whole string's contribution n; z[0] here is deliberately zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Clip a reused value at the right boundary of the current box.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not count only distinct suffixes or distinct matching prefix strings.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a wide enough integer in fixed-width languages for a quadratic-size sum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Test empty, one-character, all-identical and no-repetition inputs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the Z-array itself to expose the individual suffix scores.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Match a pattern against text with a Z-array and a separator guaranteed not to occur in either.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find all prefix lengths that also occur at selected positions by inspecting Z-values.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
